WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:07.960
My goodness! What is happening? Sam Altman has&nbsp;
resigned as the CEO of OpenAI. And we’ll talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:15.280
about this new AI tool too, which will be the&nbsp;
future of music creation, according to DeepMind.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:21.400
But firing Sam Altman? Really?&nbsp;
Why? Everything seemed so good!

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:27.320
This isn’t news that you expect to hear after&nbsp;
a the first OpenAI developer conference,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:34.640
where they announced GPT-4 Turbo, and perhaps&nbsp;
the first AI app store in a historic moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:43.080
and more. And in the meantime, work on GPT-5&nbsp;
is also underway. They also created GPTBot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:48.680
a web crawler as a preparation for&nbsp;
GPT-5. OpenAI also needs to raise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:54.920
more money than the 10 billion they have from&nbsp;
Microsoft, and work is underway on that too.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:01:01.280
And at that moment, the news just drops&nbsp;
out of the sky: Sam Altman has been asked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:08.640
by the board to step down. Wow. And what&nbsp;
happened then was absolute pandemonium.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:13.120
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:17.480
So, what happened then? Well,&nbsp;
shortly after, Greg Brockman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:22.120
co-founder and president of OpenAI also resigned.

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:29.400
And that’s not all, then, shortly after, three&nbsp;
high-level researchers also resigned. Clearly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:34.200
there are big disagreements within the&nbsp;
company as to how they should proceed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:40.400
What I hear is that one side would like to take&nbsp;
a slower, more aligned approach, while the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:46.680
is more interested in moving more quickly. It is&nbsp;
very difficult to say which is the right approach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:53.160
especially in the presence of this pace of&nbsp;
progress that you see here on Two Minute Papers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.160 --> 00:02:00.520
Sometimes you see a paper that is kinda okay,&nbsp;
but a bit like a toy, like the DALL-E 2 text to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:09.240
image AI. And then one or two more works more down&nbsp;
the line, DALL-E 3, an absolute powerhouse drops.

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:13.920
Also, that’s not all. OpenAI is a&nbsp;
non-profit entity, as it has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:22.040
created by its founders. But it is a for profit&nbsp;
entity too. Now I hear you asking, Doctor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:29.400
what is going on here? How does that happen? You&nbsp;
see, both are true. OpenAI is a non-profit entity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:36.320
that owns the for-profit entity. And the board&nbsp;
of directors controls the top level. Clearly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:42.520
there are differences in opinion whether that&nbsp;
is a good thing or not. Is OpenAI going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:49.160
become a big trillion dollar company, or is it&nbsp;
going to be a small and scrappy startup with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:54.640
these miracle AI systems? Differences&nbsp;
in opinion in there too, I am sure.

00:02:54.640 --> 00:03:01.640
Now, in the meantime, Microsoft, major investor&nbsp;
in OpenAI - they are likely as surprised by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:08.680
this as we are. But they have billions of&nbsp;
dollars invested here and they are not happy.

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:15.280
Especially because of what happened next. Next,&nbsp;
Altman said that he is ready for the next venture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:21.000
presumably with his co-founder Greg Brockman&nbsp;
and a bunch of researchers by his side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:25.880
I expected that an announcement will be&nbsp;
made about the next chapter for them.

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:32.200
And then, another bombshell news appears:&nbsp;
presumably due to the wishes of investors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:40.960
and Microsoft, Altman is in talks again to be&nbsp;
reinstated as CEO just about a day later. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:46.960
what could happen here? Well, option one, if that&nbsp;
happens, Altman would be back and would likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:55.280
be way more powerful than before. I can’t imagine&nbsp;
Microsoft not asking for at least a board seat, or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.280 --> 00:04:02.280
perhaps even an entirely new board may be created.
Or, option two, otherwise, Sam might create a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:09.000
competitor to OpenAI, and I am sure there will&nbsp;
be investors who are eager to be a part of it.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:13.880
But wait, it is still not done.&nbsp;
The talks have already started,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:20.720
and Altman was ambivalent about coming back.&nbsp;
Not so sure. A key deadline was missed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:26.320
which may trigger the mass resignation&nbsp;
of even more scientists at OpenAI.

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:32.840
So what is going to happen now? Who really&nbsp;
knows, the story is still ongoing. So far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:37.200
this is absolute pandemonium.&nbsp;
There are likely intense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:42.000
and I mean intense talks in&nbsp;
the situation room right now.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:48.320
And in the meantime, DeepMind showcased an&nbsp;
amazing new music creation AI where you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:54.760
